A virtual tabletop, but system-agnostic. The program knows about space, sight,
things, and who is allowed to touch what. It does not know what a saving throw
is. Rules are something you load, not something compiled in.

The server is written in C. Users each connect to a different port. You run the
client program with an IP and a port, it connects, and you view it in your
browser, localhost:12345 style. But you're not hosting it per se -- the server
is hosted on the server computer. They can connect just the same.

I want to make the art dynamic. More like a video game and less like a .png that
you move tokens around on.

Who is at the table, and what can they touch?

GM plus players, and the GM has secrets. But there can be multiple GMs. One
server, but players can connect as a player or a DM, and they can control
certain types of units.

Players just control one, wasd style. Commanders control several, usually weaker
but not always, RTS style. GMs command the entire map. There can be commanders
that control abstract things like the dungeon, or the forest, or the taverns in
town, and they can move stuff around like coffee cups and goblin patrols.

The players can control up to an entire party at once, but generally not more,
and specifically their viewpoint is limited to what's in front of them.

What is the interesting part -- the part actually worth designing?

All of it, which is the honest answer and also the difficult one. The map with
its sight and fog and walls. The rules layer. Content generation. Shared state
and sync across machines. And the dynamic picture, which is the one that was not
on the list and had to be written in by hand.

